    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Rory M. Butcher\ndate: 2025-01-01\n---\nRory M. Butcher\n\n- Email: hyrdmb@leeds.ac.uk\n\n- Thesis title: The Fencible Regiments in Britain, 1793-1802.\n\n- Supervisors: Prof. Kevin Linch, Dr Pete Maw\n\nProfile\n\nI began my academic career at the University of Wales Trinity St David, Lampeter, where I completed my undergraduate degree in Conflict and War in 2018. I then moved to the University of Kent to complete my master's degree in Imperial History. In the time after graduating, I worked in an education institution and the civil service before relocating to Leeds for my doctorate. My MA dissertation entitled 'Preparatory and Apparently Easy Service': The Fencible Regiments in Britain, 1793\u20131802 introduced me to the home defence apparatus which operated during the French Revolutionary Wars; I have continued working on this topic for my PhD thesis, and other publications. Alongside my doctoral research, I am currently receiving a three-year Fellowship from the Army Records Society to support the production of an edited collection on the fencible regiments, scheduled for publication in 2026.\n\nAwards\n\n- Army Records Society Fellowship Scheme, 2022-2025\n\n- Second Prize, British Commission for Military History \"3 Minute Thesis\" Competition, 2023\n\n- Society for Army Historical Research Minor University Research Grant, 2022\n\n- Professor Daniel Dawson Prize for History, 2018\n\nBook Reviews\n\n-\n\n'No Want of Courage: The British Army in Flanders, 1795\u20131795, by R. N. W. Thomas', Journal of the Society for Army Historical Research, 101.406 (Autumn 2023), 256-7\n\nConference Papers\n\n- 'Put upon equall footing?' Interbranch Relations and Perceptions in UK Home Defence, 1793-1802', The Eighth Wellington Congress, University of Southampton (2025)\n\n- 'Done the Correct Way'? Spectrums of Authenticity and Accuracy in UK Historical Reenactment of c.1750-1870', Playing War: Simulations, Games, Exercises, and the Representations of Military Force and Violence, Universit\u00e4t Regensburg (2024)\n\n- 'I was obliged to go to please the laird': Enlistment Data and Motivations in the Fencible Regiments, 1793-1802',"
